## 4.2 Topological insulator
Topological insulator (TI) is a topological nontrivial insulator with conducting edge/surface states on the boundary of bulk insulator originated from the topologies [84,85]. The lattice symmetry and spin-orbit coupling (SOC) are two essential ingredients for TIs. Up to now, the reported TIs are almost all based on inorganic materials. 2D TIs, also known as quantum spin Hall (QSH) insulators [86], have shown great potentials in spintronics and quantum computer. 2D organometallic frameworks possessing tunable electronic structures and various topological structures are good candidates for 2D organic TIs.
The latest theoretical work has revealed the possibility of utilizing 2D organometallic frameworks as TIs. For example, triphenyl-metal compound, ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{C}}_{6}{\mathrm{H}}_{5})_{3}$ , with honeycomb lattice of threefold rotational symmetry, was proposed as one new organic TI [87]. In 2013, Liu et al. [88] reported the nontrivial topological states in 2D $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BHT})_{2}$ structure with Kagome lattice (Figure 8(a, b)). The first-principle calculations disclosed the Kagome band structure with SOC around the Fermi level, consisting of one flat band above two Dirac bands. Nevertheless, precise doping is required in the functional process of TI. Yang et al. [89] found the QSH effect and $Z_{2}$ metallic states in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ monolayer, which were determined by electron-doped concentrations. For 2D $\scriptstyle{c}$ - MOFs, the electron-doping level could be controlled by modulation of the oxidation state. 2D $c$ -MOFs are advantageous for TIs due to the availability of diverse proposed structures and controllable redox states.
The band energy and spin electron structure can be controlled through the variation of redox state, which could be realized by doping guest molecules into the frameworks. For example, 7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinododimethane (TCNQ) represents a typical reductant while $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ is an ideal oxidant guest. The incorporation of proper dopant could effectively increase the charge transport ability [90]. In 2013, Nishihara et al. [91] reported the $\pi$ -conjugated nickel bis(dithiolene) complex (NiDT) nanosheets synthesized by the bottom-up interfacial reaction between BHT and Ni(II) salt (Figure 8 (c)). In addition, the redox states of NiDT were controlled by guest dopants. The as-prepared NiDT nanosheets possessed an average oxidation number of $-3/4$ . Interestingly, the pristine NiDT could be either reduced by NaTCNQ or oxidized by tris(4-bromophenyl)aminium hexachloroantimonate (Figure 8(d)) [92]. The S 2s X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) revealed the oxidation numbers of $^{-1}$ and 0 for the reduced NiDT and oxidized NiDT, respectively. The conductivity of oxidized NiDT measured by van der Pawn method was up to $1.6{\times}10^{2}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $300\mathrm{K}$ , which is significantly higher than that of pristine NiDT $(2.8\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ . The photoelectron emission spectroscopy (PES) and band structure calculation indicated the metallic nature of the pristine NiDT and oxidized NiDT, whereas the electrical conductivity measurement showed semiconductor behavior possibly attributed to the disorder and defects in the frameworks. The controllability of the oxidation states makes such 2D $\pi$ -conjugated nanosheets as unique and promising TIs.
The heavy-atom effect from the SOC also plays an important role in TIs. In 2019, one platinum analogue, bis(dithiolato)platinum (PtDT), was synthesized at the two-phase interface [93]. Upon oxidation by $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , the poor conductivity for pristine PtDT (ca. $10^{-7}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}},$ ) was increased to $0.39\mathrm{\bar{S}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The band structure calculations showed that PtDT single layer possessed a band gap of about $0.095\mathrm{eV}$ at the $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ similar to NiDI and a wider Dirac gap of $0.054\mathrm{eV}$ than NiDT, which indicated the potential of PtDT as 2D TI at room temperature.
## 5 Challenges and opportunities
Up to date, over 20,000 MOFs have been reported and applied in gas storage and separation, biological and chemical sensor, heterocatalysis, etc. [36]. In contrast, 2D $c$ -MOFs, a subgroup of 2D materials, are just in its infancy but already opened a new avenue for electronics and spintronics due to their highly conjugated 2D framework structures. With the increasing insights into the intrinsic electrical, magnetic and quantum properties, various 2D $c$ -MOFs have been explored as good multifunctional candidates for semiconductor, metal, superconductor, topological insulator and porous magnet. However, both opportunities and challenges coexist on the way toward the fabrication, characterization and applications of 2D $c$ -MOFs.
(1) The greatest synthetic challenge stems from the control of ordering and stacked layers in 2D frameworks. Ordered structures of 2D $c$ -MOFs could reduce barriers for charge or spin transport process, leading to outstanding properties for electronics and spintronics. Two approaches have been developed to synthesize 2D MOFs: the top-down methods and the bottom-up methods [94]. The bottom-up solution preparation methods for 2D $c$ -MOFs are more efficient, convenient and cost-effective. However, most reported samples are obtained as polycrystalline powders with short range order. To synthesize large single-crystals, selection of proper additives, solvent and metal precursors, and control of feeding sequence, concentration, atmosphere, pressure and reaction time should be precisely optimized. Furthermore, the charge or spin transport properties are relevant to different layers. As 2D nanomaterials, only few 2D $c$ -MOFs have been prepared as nanosheets with highly crystalline order and atomic thickness. Therefore, the understanding of the intrinsic properties of the single-layer 2D $c$ -MOFs is still limited. For example, the clear illustration of the second superconducting phase of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BHT is missing [83]. To synthesize high quality single/few-layer 2D $c$ -MOFs, some efficient preparation methods are expected to be developed, such as exfoliation and liquid-air interfacial synthesis. Particularly, chemical vapor deposition approaches will offer great opportunities to directly grow atom-thick 2D $c$ -MOFs on specific substrates.
(2) Although 2D $c$ -MOFs have presented novel electrical and magnetic properties, the intrinsic physical mechanism of charge/spin transport among the intralayer and interlayer in 2D $c$ -MOFs has remained elusive. Temperature-dependent conductivity measurement is most commonly employed to evaluate the metallic or semiconductive nature of 2D $\scriptstyle{c}$ - MOFs. However, it is not proper for evaluating the intrinsic electrical property for granular samples. Deeper investigations into transport mechanisms require joint efforts both in experimental measurements and theoretical calculations. First, besides the conventional conductivity measurements, further characterizations on charge carriers and electronic structures using flash photolysis time-resolved microwave conductivity (FP-TRMC), TRTS, UPS, Hall effect, and Seebeck effect measurements will be conducive to understand the charge transport mechanism. In addition, the crystal structure is closely related to transport pathways. As for the polycrystalline powder samples, powder X-ray diffraction (XRD), X-ray absorption fine structure spectra and highresolution transmission electron microscopy are usually performed assisted by theoretical simulations to determine the detailed structures of 2D $c$ -MOFs. On the other hand, the crystal structures of single-crystal samples can be accurately characterized by single-crystal diffraction techniques. Indeed, single-crystals with well-defined structures are good candidates to investigate the in-plane and out-of-plane transport pathways. Thus, the growth and measurement of single-crystals of 2D $c$ -MOFs and molecular model compounds are critical for elucidating the charge or spin transport mechanism. Moreover, theoretical calculations are necessary to unveil the remarkable physical effects of 2D $c$ - MOFs. For example, DFT calculations can be employed to predict the electronic band structures, further revealing anisotropic electrical properties.
(3) There are several limitations and challenges for 2D $c$ - MOFs in terms of electronic and spintronic applications. Akin to most common MOFs, the inferior stability of the coordination bonds limits their applications in harsh conditions, such as high temperature and strong acidic/alkaline conditions. Due to the existence of the redox functional groups of ligands or metal centers, the frameworks are sensitive to air in some cases. Moreover, the immature film processing technique impedes the development of 2D $c$ - MOFs-based devices. Most reported 2D $c$ -MOFs films are composed of small granular nanoparticles. The ill-defined interfaces of thicker polycrystalline films bring huge challenges for 2D $c$ -MOFs when integrated into electronic devices and hardly to achieve the intrinsic performance.
Up to now, the performances of 2D $c$ -MOFs are still modest, compared to the state-of-the-art inorganic 2D materials, which exhibit superconductivity at high temperature or magnetic ordering even at room temperature. There are two key ingredients for designing 2D $c$ -MOFs with extended $\pi\cdot$ -conjugation: the redox-active groups (i.e., $-\mathrm{OH}$ , $-\mathrm{SH}$ , $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}})$ and the conjugated ligand cores. 2D $c$ -MOFs, with strong bonding interactions in plane and weak intermolecular interactions out of plane, exhibit large conductivity and high magnetic ordering temperature.
To overcome the limitations and further improve the properties of 2D $c$ -MOFs, more efforts should be devoted for future developments. (i) The synthesis of new 2D $c$ -MOFs with novel structures will be continuously emphasized. Increasing the conjugation of ligands is vital to improve the charge transport and spin interactions, thus leading to decent conductivity/mobility and $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ . Besides the 3d transition metal centers, the implementation of lanthanide or $4\mathrm{d}/5\mathrm{d}$ metals might introduce new functions for 2D $c$ -MOFs, especially like high-performance magnets. Furthermore, constructing frameworks with new topologies are expected for exploring novel topological electron states, such as 2D superconductor, TI and Dirac semimetals. The functionalization of conjugated ligands can increase the active sites and redox activity of 2D $c$ -MOFs, and thus improving their performances for energy storage, sensors etc. (ii) Optimized methods for high crystalline crystals/films can improve the charge/spin transport ability and stability for broader applications. In addition, doping and post-synthetic modification also play specific roles in controlling the electronic structures of the final frameworks. (iii) The device fabrication procedures must be improved for future electronic and spintronic applications. Herein, some efficient film processing techniques are emphasized like interfacial synthesis, liquid phase epitaxy, electrochemical deposition and solvo/hydrothermal methods. The preparation of MOF thin-films, and heterostructured COF-MOF or MOF-MOF composite films will produce complex functions, and thus providing new opportunities for multifunctional devices. In the near future, we envision that new achievements of 2D $c$ -MOFs will be realized at the front of chemistry, physics, material science and nanotechnologies, especially in the fields of electronics, spintronics, and high-performance quantum materials.
